xgis 1

/* #2240 — `fill-translate` on a SOLID fill, as a BACKEND-PARITY subject.
 *
 * Purpose-built. The defect is that the WebGL2 fills arm (renderFillsRhi)
 * hardcodes the fill_translate_x/y uniform slots to 0 for any non-pattern fill,
 * so an authored fill-translate reaches the shader on WebGPU and is silently
 * dropped on WebGL2. No shipped basemap in this repo authors the property on a
 * plain fill (OFM uses it only on building-top roofs, which need the network),
 * so a gate pointed at a real style would measure nothing.
 *
 * The witness is the SAME scene captured on WebGPU and on ?forcegl2=1. Before
 * the fix the arms differed by construction — one applied the offset, the other
 * did not — and that difference WAS the bug. Agreement alone is not the gate,
 * though: it is satisfiable by breaking the good arm, so the spec measures each
 * backend's quad centroid against the authored offset first and asserts
 * agreement on top (_fill-translate-parity-gate.spec.ts).
 *
 * One layer, one opaque colour, no stroke: the measurement is a pixel diff over
 * a solid quad, and a stroke would draw through the LINE pipeline, whose vertex
 * shader reads the same two slots (line.ts:1457-1462, the polygon-outline path)
 * — mixing both consumers into one arm would make a red ambiguous about which
 * one moved.
 *
 * The offset is large (60 CSS px on x) so the diff is unmistakable at any DPR
 * and cannot be mistaken for an antialiasing edge.
 *
 * Geometry is pushed via setSourceData after run() — this container has no
 * network egress.
 */

source poly { type: geojson }

layer fill_translated {
  source: poly
  | fill-#ff3b30 stroke-0 fill-translate-x-60 fill-translate-y-0
}
